    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> broke from Spanish rule and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1811 and was isolated for decades under the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gaspar-rodriguez-de-francia">José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia</a>.</li><li>The War of the Triple Alliance of 1864–1870 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> killed a large share of its people, by some estimates over half.</li><li>It won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> in 1932–1935, lived under Stroessner from 1954 to 1989 and has been a democracy since the constitution of 1992.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>’s Presidente Hayes department and the town of Villa Hayes are named after the US president Rutherford B. Hayes, whose arbitration award of 1878 gave Paraguay the disputed Chaco.</p></aside>
    </section>

